---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CombineImages from '@/components/tools/image-edit/CombineImages.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="photo-collage-maker">
  <CombineImages client:load defaultLayout="grid" collage={true} />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Building a grid collage</h2>
    <p>
      The photo collage maker opens on a two-column grid with a 16 px gap and 12 px rounded corners, a
      layout that looks designed at once. Three controls shape the result: the number of
      columns, the gap, and the background colour behind the gaps. Every cell is the size of your
      largest photo, and each image is fitted inside its cell without cropping or distortion, so
      portrait and landscape shots can share a grid without anything being squashed.
    </p>

    <h2>Choosing the column count</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Photos</th><th>Columns</th><th>Result</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>A pair, side by side</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>3 or 1</td><td>A strip: wide for a banner, tall for a story</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td>2</td><td>The classic square quad</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6</td><td>3</td><td>Two rows of three; good for a 3:2 print</td></tr>
          <tr><td>9</td><td>3</td><td>Square grid, the Instagram-panel look</td></tr>
          <tr><td>12–16</td><td>4</td><td>Contact sheet; consider capping input width first</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      As a general rule, take the square root of your photo count and round: four photos want two
      columns, nine want three, sixteen want four. If you break that rule on purpose (one column, or
      one row), you get a story strip or a banner.
    </p>

    <h2>Gaps, corners and background</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Gap. Roughly 1–2% of the collage width reads as a designed margin. Zero gap gives a seamless mosaic, which suits photos of the same subject; a large gap in white gives a Polaroid-wall feel.</li>
      <li>Corner rounding. 8–16 px is contemporary. It applies per photo, so the background colour shows at the corners. Pick a background you want to see.</li>
      <li>Background. White for printing, black for a gallery look, or your brand colour for social. Switch on transparency and export PNG if the collage will be placed on an unknown background.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Order and arrangement</h2>
    <p>
      The grid fills row by row, left to right, in the order of the file chips above the preview.
      Drag the chips to rearrange. A useful trick for mixed orientations: put your one landscape
      photo first so it fills the top-left cell, and put the portraits after it. The largest image
      sets the cell size, so the layout stays even.
    </p>

    <h2>Collages for print</h2>
    <p>
      Export PNG for the sharpest result, then tag the physical size with
      <a href="/change-image-dpi">Change Image DPI</a>. For a 6 × 4 inch print at 300 DPI you want a
      1800 × 1200 pixel collage; the pixel dimensions of your canvas are shown above the preview, so
      you can check before you export. If the collage comes out smaller than that, your source
      photos are the limit. Enlarging afterwards only invents pixels.
    </p>

    <h2>Memory, not policy, is the only limit</h2>
    <p>
      There is no cap on how many photos you can add, because there is no server to protect: the
      canvas is built in your own browser. The practical ceiling is device memory. A sixteen-photo
      grid of 12-megapixel images is a canvas of roughly 200 million pixels, which is around 800 MB
      of RAM before encoding. That is fine on a desktop, but likely to fail on an older phone. If your browser
      gives up, shrink the inputs first with <a href="/resize-image">Resize Image</a>, or build the
      collage in two halves and merge them with
      <a href="/merge-images-side-by-side">Merge Images Side by Side</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Related tools</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/combine-images">Combine Images</a> for the plain side-by-side and stacked layouts,
      <a href="/add-text-to-image">Add Text to Image</a> to title the finished collage, and
      <a href="/compress-image-to-1mb">compress to 1 MB</a> if it needs to fit an upload limit.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
